    "body": "## Overview\n\nOn a host exposing both NVIDIA and Intel GPUs, an XPU wheel currently fails to\nstart: the CUDA and XPU platform plugins both activate (raising \"Only one\nplatform plugin can be activated\"), and Triton disables itself on seeing\nmultiple active drivers. This makes an XPU build behave correctly on such\nmixed-driver nodes.\n\n## Claims\n\n- An XPU build on a node that also has a working NVIDIA/NVML driver now resolves\n  to the `xpu` platform instead of erroring with \"Only one platform plugin can\n  be activated\".\n- Triton stays enabled on multi-driver nodes by honoring `TRITON_DEFAULT_BACKEND`\n  (the same env var Triton's own `_create_driver()` uses) to pin the active\n  driver, instead of disabling itself.\n\n## Validation\n\nValidated end-to-end on an XPU wheel running on a node that also exposes a\nworking NVIDIA/NVML driver (the exact mixed-driver case this PR targets).\nMultiple full `vllm serve` + benchmark runs across TP4 and PP2/TP8 configs\n(moonshotai/Kimi-K3), each launched with `TRITON_DEFAULT_BACKEND=intel`, which\nexercises both halves of the change.\n\nPlatform-plugin guard fires and resolution lands on `xpu`:\n\n    DEBUG [platforms/__init__.py:70] CUDA platform is not available because vLLM is built for XPU.\n    DEBUG [platforms/__init__.py:290] Automatically detected platform xpu.\n\nServe command exports the pinned Triton backend so Triton stays enabled:\n\n    TRITON_DEFAULT_BACKEND=intel $(which vllm) serve moonshotai/Kimi-K3 ... -tp 4 ...\n\nObserved on runs dated 2026-10-05 through 2026-10-13.\n\nCI note: upstream CI has no node exposing both NVIDIA and Intel drivers, so this\npath cannot be exercised in CI; it was validated locally as above.\n\n## Details\n\n- `cuda_platform_plugin()` declines early (`return None`) when the wheel is an\n  XPU build (`vllm_version_matches_substr(\"xpu\")`), mirroring the existing\n  CPU-build guard, so only the XPU plugin activates.\n- `triton_utils/importing.py` reads `TRITON_DEFAULT_BACKEND` and, when set and\n  that backend's driver is active, pins it as the single active driver. Builds\n  on the already-merged #53530 (`name != \"cpu\"` guard).\n\nNot a duplicate — searched upstream (no matching PR/issue): `platform plugin xpu\ncuda`, `TRITON_DEFAULT_BACKEND` (only merged #53530), `cuda_platform_plugin xpu\nbuild`, `both NVIDIA Intel multiple drivers triton`, and issue search\n`Only one platform plugin can be activated xpu`.\n\nTotal: 2 files changed, +21 lines.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
